Here's the complete migration from ASP.NET Web Forms to ASP.NET Core 8 MVC:

## Migrated Code

```csharp
// ASP.NET Core 8 — ReportController.cs
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc;

public class ReportController : Controller
{
    // POST /Report/Export
    [HttpPost]
    public IActionResult Export()
    {
        var data = GenerateReport();

        // Return a file result — no Response.End needed.
        // The framework handles Content-Disposition and streaming.
        return File(
            System.Text.Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes(data),
            "text/csv",
            "report.csv");
    }

    // POST /Report/Redirect
    [HttpPost]
    public IActionResult RedirectAction()
    {
        if (!User.Identity?.IsAuthenticated ?? true)
        {
            return Redirect("/login");
        }

        try
        {
            ProcessRequest();
            return Redirect("/success");
        }
        catch (Exception ex)
        {
            LogError(ex);
            return Redirect("/error");
        }
    }
}
```

## What Changed and Why

| Old (.NET Framework) | New (ASP.NET Core 8) | Reason |
|---|---|---|
| `Response.Clear/Write/End` | `return File(...)` | Action results replace manual response writing. `Response.End()` called `Thread.Abort` internally — that throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` in modern .NET. |
| `Response.AddHeader(...)` | Handled by `File()` result | The framework sets `Content-Disposition` automatically. |
| `Response.Redirect(url, true)` | `return Redirect(url)` | The `true` (endResponse) param triggered `Thread.Abort`. In Core, just return a redirect result — execution stops naturally. |
| `catch (ThreadAbortException)` | **Removed entirely** | No `Thread.Abort` means no `ThreadAbortException`. This catch block was only needed because `Response.Redirect` threw it as a side effect. |
| `Controller` (Web Forms) | `Controller` (MVC) | Same name, different base class (`Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc.Controller`). |

## Key Pitfalls

1. **Don't call `HttpContext.Response.Body.Write()` then try to redirect** — once you start writing the response body in Core, you can't change headers or status code. Always use action results.

2. **`User.Identity` can be null** in Core if no authentication middleware is configured. Use null-conditional (`?.`) access.

3. **No implicit `Thread.Abort` control flow** — in Framework, `Response.Redirect(url, true)` prevented any code after it from running. In Core, code after `return` won't run, but if you forget `return`, execution continues.

4. **Don't swallow `OperationCanceledException`** — if you have long-running work, pass `HttpContext.RequestAborted` as a `CancellationToken` and let the exception propagate:

```csharp
[HttpPost]
public async Task<IActionResult> LongExport(CancellationToken cancellationToken)
{
    // Framework binds HttpContext.RequestAborted automatically
    var data = await GenerateReportAsync(cancellationToken);
    return File(Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes(data), "text/csv", "report.csv");
}
```

5. **Web Forms event handlers → HTTP verbs** — `btnExport_Click` becomes an `[HttpPost]` action. Wire up the form with `<form asp-action="Export">` instead of server-side button events.
